WEBVTT

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.570
Our fitted design has forty six
columns including the intercept,

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.270
but its measured rank is forty two.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.930
Several columns repeat relationships
already expressible by others.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:17.210
In raw coordinates,
every row activates exactly one hour,

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.790
weekday and month.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.470
Each complete indicator group
therefore sums to the intercept column.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.690
In these training records,

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.010
working day also equals the Monday
through Friday indicators minus holiday.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.850
The independent audit checks that
extra relation on this export.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.630
Standardization changes coordinates
without removing those dependencies.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.780
The training data can determine a fitted
function on supported inputs without

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.570
assigning a unique value
to every coefficient.

00:00:50.942 --> 00:00:53.852
Consider a simple
equivalent parameterization.

00:00:54.302 --> 00:00:59.092
Add ten to every raw hour coefficient
and subtract ten from the intercept.

00:00:59.722 --> 00:01:04.192
Every valid hour vector has
exactly one active hour indicator.

00:01:04.742 --> 00:01:07.372
The added ten and subtracted ten cancel.

00:01:07.802 --> 00:01:09.332
The forecast stays unchanged.

00:01:10.322 --> 00:01:13.752
That gives two different coefficient
arrays for the same predictions.

00:01:14.802 --> 00:01:18.582
Requiring two solvers to print identical
arrays would reject an equivalent

00:01:18.582 --> 00:01:19.592
representation.

00:01:20.322 --> 00:01:23.392
We compare their predictions
and objective values instead.

00:01:23.962 --> 00:01:28.362
This argument also cautions against
interpreting a single coefficient as a

00:01:28.362 --> 00:01:29.832
unique causal effect.

00:01:30.242 --> 00:01:34.812
Its numerical value depends on the chosen
representation and the other columns.

00:01:37.025 --> 00:01:41.155
The reference first uses the
same saved training scaler.

00:01:41.405 --> 00:01:45.805
It appends ones and solves the training
least squares problem with a stable

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:47.095
numerical routine.

00:01:47.625 --> 00:01:51.195
We do not invert a normal
matrix that may be singular.

00:01:51.805 --> 00:01:53.575
The routine returns parameters,

00:01:54.005 --> 00:01:55.875
rank and singular values.

00:01:56.625 --> 00:02:00.195
Its residual summary can be
empty for a dependent design,

00:02:00.525 --> 00:02:05.185
so we explicitly calculate prediction
errors instead of calling that empty array

00:02:05.185 --> 00:02:06.415
zero loss.

00:02:07.225 --> 00:02:11.825
The iterative and reference predictions
differ by less than one millionth of a

00:02:11.825 --> 00:02:14.915
rental on observed training
and validation inputs.

00:02:15.445 --> 00:02:18.655
Their training objectives agree
to the displayed precision.

00:02:19.225 --> 00:02:21.195
That supports numerical correctness,

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:24.695
while forecast usefulness
remains a separate measurement.

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:28.997
To inspect one hour,

00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:32.217
convert the standardized
model back to raw coordinates.

00:02:32.687 --> 00:02:35.477
Divide each weight by
its saved feature scale.

00:02:36.027 --> 00:02:39.687
Then subtract the mean weighted
contribution from the standardized

00:02:39.687 --> 00:02:42.417
intercept to obtain the raw intercept.

00:02:42.807 --> 00:02:45.657
This conversion preserves
the same function.

00:02:46.327 --> 00:02:49.637
In raw coordinates,
inactive indicators are zero again,

00:02:49.927 --> 00:02:53.697
so Monday at eight can be read as
an intercept plus four adjustments.

00:02:54.547 --> 00:02:58.297
The implementation checks equality
between both coordinate systems.

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:01.817
A constant training column uses scale one,

00:03:01.887 --> 00:03:06.517
which protects arithmetic but supplies
no learned evidence for unseen variation.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:10.437
This is a readable decomposition
of the saved representation,

00:03:10.887 --> 00:03:13.517
not a unique account
of what causes demand.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:20.500
The saved raw intercept is about one
hundred and forty three point five seven.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:25.960
The hour eight coefficient adds about
one hundred and nineteen point five six.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.240
Monday adds three point seven three,

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:33.130
January subtracts ninety
two point seven nine,

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:37.080
and working day adds about
zero point seven one.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:42.660
Their sum is one hundred and seventy
four point seven eight rentals per hour.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.660
This equals the standardized
full vector calculation.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:51.660
If we shift every hour coefficient
and compensate in the intercept,

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.700
that total stays unchanged

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:57.040
even though this displayed
decomposition changes.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:01.390
Check the fitted function and its
supported inputs before drawing

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.240
conclusions from individual
dependent coefficients.

